  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nTonight: Fog Potential\n\nWeak return southerly flow is spreading eastward across the \nregion this afternoon behind the surface high pressure cell that\nbrought our sunny skies today. This will aid to slowly bring \nslightly higher dewpoints northward over the next 24 hours. This\nincrease in moisture is progged to either bring another round \nof fog or low stratus later tonight into Friday morning. Given \nthe advective component of this scenario, stratus may be \nslightly more favored over fog, or it may be a case where the \nlower valleys see stratus and the upper ridgetops see fog. As \nwith this morning, dense fog is possible into northeast Iowa and\nsouthwest Wisconsin. This fog/stratus looks to burn off by mid \nto late morning ahead of our approaching cold front.\n\nFriday Afternoon-Evening: Storm Details\n\nA compact shortwave rotating around a broad, quasi-stationary \nsouth-central Canadian low ushers a cold front out of the \nDakotas later this evening, with the boundary bifurcating \nMinnesota from SW to NE by midday Friday. Forecast soundings \nahead of the front show a modestly stout cap in place that may \nbe difficult to breach given the weakness of the attendant upper\nlevel support. Most CAMs do develop some form of convection \nalong the front, but with varying degrees of location and \ncoverage. In fact, the 12Z HRRR really struggles to develop much\nfor any convection across the local area--keeping it further \nnorth where forcing may be slightly stronger and inhibition \nless.\n\nShould surbace-based convection occur, there will be 1000-1500 \nJ/kg of MLCAPE to realize and a mean cross-boundary steering \nflow that could keep cells more discrete. That being said, the \ncomplexities of the 2-6-km hodographs--plus the forcing being \nalong the front--will likely result in cells growing upscale to \nlines or clusters in fairly short order and limiting any hail \nthreat. Pockets of strong winds would be the main hazards with \nthese storms\n\nSaturday Night - Tuesday: Periodic Rounds of Storms\n\nThe culprit cold front bringing tomorrow afternoon/evening's \nstorms slides to around the I-80 corridor in Iowa/Illinois by \nSaturday morning. However, with the upper tropospheric pattern \ntransitioning more to a quasi-NW/zonal flow, the next subtle \nripple in the flow helps promote lee cyclogenesis along the \nFront Range that lifts the baroclinic zone northward. The medium\nrange solutions differ considerably on the degree of convective\ninitiation along this lifting warm front between Saturday night\nand Sunday, but deterministic forecast soundings from the GFS \nshow a rather primed setup with the potential for organized \nconvection by Sunday afternoon. The question becomes whether \nstorms can break through the cap and exactly where/when that \noccurs, a tough question to answer at this range in the \nforecast. With the mid to upper level steering flow having a \nsubstantial boundary parallel component, training storms and \nlocally heavy rain would be a concern. \n\nMoving into early next week, this baroclinic zone slowly sags \nsouthward, its exact position driven by how convection unfolds \nover each successive day. The Monday into Tuesday timeframe \ncould feature an MCS tracking along the boundary as depicted in\nvarious ensembles, but location and timing are highly variable \nand there is not much else to hand one's hat on.\n\nWith the increasing risk for convection each day and weak north\nto east flow to the north of the boundary, the temperature \nforecast for the weekend and early next week will feature highs \nat or slightly cooler than average.",
